<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Werla</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Werla was a royal palace (Pfalz) of the Ottonian kings on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oker">Oker</a> river near Schladen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a> took refuge there from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> raid in 926 before agreeing a nine-year truce, and Otto I and his successors held assemblies there. It declined after the Salian kings favoured nearby Goslar, and excavations have uncovered its walls and buildings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/werla.jpg" alt="Werla" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Okernick · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WerlaWestturmVonSueden.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The palace stood on the Kreuzberg, a natural plateau 17 m high above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oker">Oker</a>, about 15 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a>, near the village of Werlaburgdorf, which was called simply <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgdorf">Burgdorf</a> until 1958. Its core was a cross-shaped fortress about 150 m across, two of its sides resting on the steep river banks, enclosed by a wall a metre thick and a moat 9 m wide. Within stood a chapel, a hall, two palace buildings, one of them heated, and an underground passage. Three baileys, protected by ditches and earthworks, brought the enclosed area to about 20 hectares.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The hill was settled in prehistoric times, and a fortified farmstead stood there in the 9th century; the palace itself was built at the beginning of the 10th. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind-of-corvey">Widukind of Corvey</a> records the stay of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a> during a Hungarian attack, when a Hungarian leader, probably Zoltán, son of Grand Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arpad">Árpád</a>, was captured, and his release bought the truce. Fourteen royal visits are recorded up to 1013, five of them by Otto the Great, and in 1002 Duke Henry IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> was accepted there as successor to Otto III.</p><p>Under Henry II the palace began to lose its place to the new palace at Goslar, which controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rammelsberg">Rammelsberg</a>, and in 1086 Henry IV leased about 400 Hufen of its estate to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim">Hildesheim</a>. Werla did not decay but grew: its baileys were enlarged and fortified, and metal and textile workshops worked in them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> came in 1180, the last emperor to do so, during his conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a>. By 1550 at the latest the site was in ruins, its stone taken by the villagers, and its memory was lost.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The site was found again in the 19th century and excavated from 1934, when the Nazis presented it as the birthplace of the First Reich and finds were dated to the Ottonian period wherever possible; further digs followed from 1957 to 1964 and from 2007. Since 2010 its walls and earthworks have been partly rebuilt, and the west tower of the core fortress fully reconstructed, as the Archäologie- und Landschaftspark Kaiserpfalz Werla, opened on 14 September 2012.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Royal palace of Werla” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Henry I and Otto I became kings, much of Saxony became royal land, administered from palaces such as Quedlinburg, Magdeburg and <b>Werla</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
